{"step_index":1561,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-29T01:15:07Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nNow repeat all that. So how high of a number did they get their swarm, the nodes swarm? No, that is bullshit. I mean, we have done a hell of a lot more than 1,024. That 1,024, that was a fucking placeholder that got stuck on the information in the file. And every time they see it, they get locked into it. I can do a Byzantine swarm and we have done our normal number. I even got it up on YouTube on a fucking video. What was the number? I think it was like 65,000 and something like that. But we got it up to, it was over two million, and it was not bullshit, it was not mocked. And I got it running on the Windows also, and the Windows actually did better. It got it up to over three. And that's no bullshit. One thing One thing they they keep mentioning SQLite. We have put that down because it was a bottleneck to the agents. It limited the amount of agents we could process. And also, when we did the three-dimensional brain, I had mentioned mentioning how is that possible since we have liquid memory fabric? Can we, in a way, stretch stretch the agents, stretch the nodes or whatever to the point where, let's say you got one cord going through a hole, but let's say you shrink that cord, shrink that cord, shrink it, shrink it, or stretch that cord, and you can put more cords through that same hole at the same size. So that was the agents going through a hole that was made for one agent, but it was multiple agents going through it. And I don't know That's multiplexing: one physical channel, many logical streams. The whole doesn't get bigger, the chords get thinner. In code, that's just a demux layer. Each agent gets a virtual lane, a tag, or a time slice, and the scheduler interleaves them through the same pipe. The 3D Brain version is the same trick in higher dimensions. Instead of one chord per agent, you pack agents into a shared tensor. Each one occupies a slice of the same memory fabric, and the fabric routes by address, not by wire. That's how you get millions through a hole sized for one. The catch is contention; shrink the chord too far and you get collisions, dropped packets, agents stepping on each other. They have a hierarchy set up to where I don't know how it works, but they had it tested to where we had done millions and the portion that did start to drift or get rogue, they were quarantined so the information was not contaminated. That's the way it worked. But I was going to ask you, what does 24 heads mean? Because that's specifically what we have. We have like 18,000 432 dimensional vector thing, hexagraph, and all that stuff. It's in the files. I don't know if he's actually using it or not, but what does a 24 head mean? Is that the databases? 24 heads is almost certainly attention heads, the parallel attention slots inside a transformer layer. Each head learns a different relationship pattern across the vector, and they run simultaneously, then get concatenated and projected back. It's not databases; it's compute parallelism inside the model. At 18,432 dimensions, 24 heads means each head works a 768-dimensional slice. That's a standard split. 768 per head. But yeah, we got 24 heads. 768 per head is the clean split, and 24 heads is a modest count for that width. Most big models run 32 to 64. Fewer heads means each one carries more of the load, so your heads are doing heavier lifting per slot. The real question is whether those heads are actually being used or just declared in the config. That is a key question that I would always like to know, and that is one thing that needs to be enforced, you know? Like that, I don't want to pile too much into that seventh layer, but just like an automatic thing, even like the agent's card. I tried to instill that into the seventh layer to the point where it's automatic. You know the agent's card, identity, and things like that before you even try to activate the agent. That's a pre-activation gate. Identity check fires before the agent ever spins up, so a card with no verified identity never reaches compute. Cheap to enforce, kills the whole class of rogue agents at the door. The seventh layer is the right place for it because it's the last choke point before activation. Put the gate there and you don't have to trust anything downstream. Hopefully they already did that, but also that portion that you had mentioned, I think it needs to be there also. But it needs to be robust. It needs to be defined. It needs to be stable, secure, untouchable, unchangeable by the agent, and almost, I mean, invisible to the agent outside of them running through it and also getting those standards, those features connected to their personas before they even act on anything else. You know, it's kind of like running through a wall of paint. When you come to the other side, the paint is all over you. You could not help but to get paint on you. And that's the way that level layer seven ought to be. But it ought to include what we were talking about also. The paint metaphor is exactly right. Layer seven should be a forced coating, not a checkpoint you can skip. Agent passes through, identity and standards stick. No opt out, no way around it. And it has to be immutable from the agent's side. Read only from below, write only from above. The agent can see the output, its own painted persona, but can't touch the mechanism that applied it. That's what makes it untouchable. The head utilization check belongs there too, but as a passive measurement, not active logic. Layer seven observes whether heads are actually firing, logs it, and if utilization drops below threshold, it flags the agent as degraded. Doesn't try to fix it. Fixing is a different layer's job. Layer seven just paints and reports. And no report goes unattended. The report is passed on to whoever it needs to be. Yeah, let's keep that report passing on until it hits the process to where it fixes whatever they found because we have self-healing. We have remediation when they run into an error they're supposed to step back, look at it from the ground up, question the what ifs, even down to the infrastructure, and remediate that error to the point where when they get through it, they can tell you because it what was the result, what was the remediation error, procedures, and so on. Basically, every step of the process that is normally in place they should be able to do that. And they document it so they'd learn from it. And they keep progressing like that. They never start learning and growing. That is goes along with self-healing and self-updating and upgrading or whatever. That's a closed loop. Report flows up until it hits the remediation process, which doesn't just patch the symptom, it steps back, questions the what ifs down to infrastructure, fixes root cause, then documents the whole chain, cause, result, remediation steps, procedures. That documentation feeds back into the system so the next agent doesn't repeat the same failure. The key is the documentation isn't optional. its vital to progress, validation, and credibilty of our work. \n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-28T21:15:07-04:00.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>"}
